<PageLayout title="Amin Samkough in “North Caucasians in Emigration in the 20th Century”">
  <p>
    <a href="/people/amin-samkough/">&larr; Amin Samkough</a>
  </p>
  <hr />

  <section id="entry">
    <h2>Dictionary entry</h2>
    <p>Original Russian:</p>
    <blockquote lang="ru">
      <p>
        САМГУГ Амин ад. (1900, Кунейтра, Сирия — 1953). Потомок черкесских
        эмигрантов второй половины ХIХ в. Жил в Дамаске, Бейруте. Организовал
        «Черкесское общество содействия просвещению и культуре» (центр —
        Сирия). Член правления Народной партии горцев Кавказа.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>English translation:</p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        SAMGUG Amin, Adyg [Circassian]. (1900, Quneitra, Syria — 1953).
        Descendant of Circassian emigrants of the second half of the 19th
        century. Lived in Damascus and Beirut. Organized the “Circassian
        Society for the Promotion of Education and Culture” (center — Syria).
        Member of the board of the People’s Party of Mountaineers of the
        Caucasus.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      <small>
        In the book’s list of abbreviations, “ад.” stands for <em>адыги</em>
        (Adyg), the collective name for the related peoples of the western and
        central North Caucasus, including Circassians.
      </small>
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="source">
    <h2>About the source</h2>
    <p>
      The entry comes from a 2020 reference book, <cite lang="ru">«Северокавказцы в эмиграции в ХХ веке. Материалы к биографическому словарю»</cite>
      (<em>North Caucasians in Emigration in the 20th Century: Materials for a
      Biographical Dictionary</em>), by I. L. Babich, T. L. Gladkova and L. A.
      Mnukhin, edited by A. N. Kozhanovsky. It was published in Moscow and
      Berlin by Direct-Media (297 pages, ISBN 978-5-4499-1574-0) and produced by
      the N. N. Miklukho-Maklai Institute of Ethnology and Anthropology of the
      Russian Academy of Sciences together with the Turgenev Russian Library in
      Paris.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is a biographical dictionary of people from the North Caucasus, or
      from North Caucasian peoples, who ended up outside the Russian Empire and
      the USSR after the 1917 revolution and the Second World War. The entries,
      arranged alphabetically, give short facts about each person’s family,
      religion and public and political life. They are drawn from archives in
      Europe, emigrant periodicals of the 1920s–1950s, family papers and
      cemetery records. The authors describe it as material for a dictionary and
      aim for entries free of ideological judgment.
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="why">
    <h2>Why Amin Samkough appears in it</h2>
    <p>
      The dictionary covers Circassians and other North Caucasians whose
      families were exiled after the Russo-Caucasian War, including those in
      Turkey and Syria. Samkough qualifies on two grounds. He was a descendant
      of Circassian emigrants, and he organized a Circassian cultural and
      educational society in Syria. He is also recorded as a board member of the
      People’s Party of Mountaineers of the Caucasus, an émigré political party
      that other entries in the book place in Paris and Istanbul in the late
      1920s and 1930s.
    </p>
    <p>
      The entry is brief and gives no source for these facts. It matches other
      accounts of his life on this site in his birth in Quneitra, his years in
      Damascus and Beirut, and the “Circassian Society for the Promotion of
      Education and Culture”. The party membership does not appear elsewhere on
      this site.
    </p>
    <p>
      The book’s name index lists him as “Самгуг А.” on page 157. The full text
      is available as a
      <a href="https://static.iea.ras.ru/news/Severokavkazcy_v_emigracii.pdf">
        PDF from the Institute of Ethnology and Anthropology
      </a>.
    </p>
  </section>
